# v210 Help Center addition for the unified browser and newly decoded HEKA modes.
_V209_UNIFIED_BROWSER_HELP = ("Browser grouping and HEKA plot modes", r"""
UNIFIED DATA CATALOG

FaradaIQ Studio v273 builds one canonical browser catalog before drawing the experiment tree. The catalog is shared by HEKA, PalmSens, VersaStudio, BioLogic, Solartron, Gamry, CSV/TXT, DRT, and other supported importers. Importer metadata is authoritative. Filenames and headers are used only when structured metadata is unavailable.

TECHNIQUE TREE

Only techniques present in the loaded bundle are shown. Each technique has consistent views:

  Technique
    Grouped by method
      Method 01 — protocol or Series title
        Point 001
          cycle, sweep, line, or processed view
    Grouped by point
      Point 001
        Method 01 — protocol or Series title
          cycle, sweep, line, or processed view

CV/LSV can also be grouped by scan rate when scan-rate metadata exists. DRT can also be grouped by analysis run and engine. HEKA techniques include an Original HEKA hierarchy view that preserves Group, Series, and Sweep order without using acquisition Series numbers as method numbers.

METHODS VERSUS POINTS

A HEKA Series index is acquisition order, not automatically a distinct scientific method. Repeated Series with the same protocol or Series title across spatial points are grouped as one method. Example:

  Linear sweep / polarization (LSV)
    Method 01 — ORR
      Point 001
      Point 002
      ...
      Point 025

TipDown and PullUp are different approach methods. Repeated TipDown Series at different points remain under one TipDown method. EIS frequency Series are assembled into one EIS method with one selectable spectrum per spatial Group/point.

SELECTION AND AUTOMATIC PLOT MODE

Selecting a technique, method, point, or leaf automatically chooses the matching plot mode. Selecting a parent plots all descendant tables, up to Max curves. Duplicate table paths shown in more than one grouping view are de-duplicated before plotting.

DEFAULT MODES

• CV, LSV, FSCV, pulse voltammetry, and I–V/Tafel: current versus potential.
• CA: current versus time.
• CP and OCP: potential versus time.
• EIS Nyquist: Z-prime versus negative Z-double-prime.
• EIS Bode magnitude: frequency versus impedance magnitude with logarithmic frequency and magnitude.
• EIS Bode phase: frequency versus phase with logarithmic frequency.
• DRT: tau versus gamma with logarithmic tau.
• Approach/retract: signal versus Z or distance when a valid coordinate exists.
• Distance calibration: calibration signal versus distance/Z when available.
• Shear-force spectra: amplitude or phase versus frequency when protocol semantics identify a frequency sweep.
• Time, X, Y, Z, and line scans: signal versus the corresponding coordinate when available.
• 2D/3D data: coordinate-aware map when complete coordinates exist.

APPROACH CURVE EXAMPLE

1. Expand Approach / retract curves.
2. Open Grouped by method.
3. Select Method 01 — TipDown, one point, or one sweep.
4. The plot mode changes to Approach curve.
5. If the exported data contain Z/distance, FaradaIQ uses it as X.
6. If no reliable coordinate is stored, FaradaIQ plots ordered acquisition time and writes a warning in the browser log. It does not invent distance.

SHEAR-FORCE EXAMPLE

Use Technique plot views to choose Amplitude / monitor signal or Phase. For a Series named SF_Spectra whose decoded X trace is a frequency sweep, the X axis is labeled Frequency / Hz. Constant-distance shear-force records use available Z/distance or time channels. The selected Series title, method, point, and sweep appear in the legend.

EIS EXAMPLE

Expand Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy, then Technique plot views. Choose Nyquist, frequency-gradient Nyquist, Bode magnitude, Bode phase, or stored-Zimag diagnostic. Each spatial spectrum remains individually selectable. The stored-sign diagnostic shows the original Zimag sign; the normal Nyquist view follows the conventional negative-Zimag display.

MISSING COORDINATES AND WARNINGS

FaradaIQ never fabricates X/Y/Z positions. When coordinates are missing or incomplete, the browser retains point order, plots an ordered trace where scientifically honest, and records a clear warning. Map modes require enough finite X, Y, and signal values. Empty technique categories remain hidden.

PLOT OPTIONS

All dedicated modes retain the standard plot controls: overlay, individual selection, legend, palette, linear/log axes, grid, manual X/Y limits, reset axes, SVG/PDF/TIFF/PNG export, and plot-recipe JSON. CV/trace view still provides I-V, I-t, E-t, Q-t, and Auto alternatives.
""")
